KG42.register("rules-49d36a77f7a7b1f96e31e20c",{"id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R005","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R005","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Counterparty matching","process_stage":"matching","market_meaning":"Grouping microgrids into trading and non-trading sets is conditional on their ability to contribute to social operating cost minimization. Scope: Identification of the trading group M′ among interconnected microgrids in the P1/P2 bargaining decomposition. Condition: Whether a microgrid can contribute to social operating cost minimization.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function matching.counterparties under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["clearing.social_welfare","matching.counterparties"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R005-E001","source":"matching.counterparties","target":"clearing.social_welfare","type":"conditional_on","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R005-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E21"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Identification of the trading group M′ among interconnected microgrids in the P1/P2 bargaining decomposition.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E21"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"e_i","symbol":"e_i","meaning":"Inter-microgrid energy trading vector of microgrid i in the optimal P1 social operating cost minimization: e_i = {e_{i,j}^t, ∀t∈T, ∀j∈M\\i}; e_{i,j}^t > 0 if i purchases from j, e_{i,j}^t \u003c 0 if i sells to j. A nonzero e_i is the source-stated criterion for membership in the trading set M′.","kind":"operational","domain":"Signed real vector over time slots t ∈ T and trading partners j ∈ M\\{i}; each component is an energy quantity (sign convention from source).","unit":null,"indices":["i","j","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E18"]},{"variable_id":"M_prime","symbol":"M′","meaning":"Set of microgrids that trade energy with at least one other microgrid in the optimal P1 solution; the trading group identified by the matching rule. Equivalently, M′ = {i ∈ M : e_i ≠ 0}.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"2^M (subsets of the interconnected microgrid set M)","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E18","v141-b-g02-E21"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"M","symbol":"M","meaning":"Set of interconnected microgrids, M = {1, ..., M}, where the source also uses M for the number of microgrids; the scope over which the trading/non-trading classification is quantified.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"M = {1, ..., M}"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e02","v141-b-g02-E17"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"Grouping microgrids into trading and non-trading sets is conditional on their ability to contribute to social operating cost minimization. Scope: Identification of the trading group M′ among interconnected microgrids in the P1/P2 bargaining decomposition. Condition: Whether a microgrid can contribute to social operating cost minimization.","expression":"M' = { i ∈ M | e_i ≠ 0 }  and  ∀i ∈ M: (i ∈ M' ⇔ e_i ≠ 0)","formalization_origin":"equivalent_translation","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties","clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":["e_i","M_prime"],"parameter_refs":["M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E21","v141-b-g02-E18","v141-b-g02-E20"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"BP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy d- trading & scheduling and trading p","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Theorem 1: The energy-trading bargaining problem NBP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy trading & scheduling and trading payment, denoted as P1 and P2, respectively. In the first step, we solve a social operating cost minimization problem P1 for the interconnected-microgrids system, which yields the optimal energy scheduling and energy trading for all microgrids. Those microgrids that trade energy with other microgrids form the set M′. In the second step, microgrids in set M′ bargain with each other to determine the energy-trading payments in P2."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p07-c1r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":7,"printed_page":"2653","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"WANG AND HUANG: INCENTIVIZING ENERGY TRADING FOR INTERCONNECTED M microgrids do not contribute to th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"microgrids do not contribute to the social cost minimization and won't benefit from energy trading. The only criterion for determining trading and non-trading microgrids is whether they can contribute to the social cost minimization. Secondly, after identifying those microgrids who do not want to participate in the energy trading, we then focus on the rest of the microgrids who trade energy. We decide a fair allocation of benefits to each of participated microgrids through the payment bargaining problem P2."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e02","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p02-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. INTERCONNECTED-MICROGRIDS SYSTEM","anchor_text":"g ts. interconnected-microgrids system and jointly optimize both We their internal power scheduling ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We consider a network of M interconnected microgrids M = {1, ..., M}. These microgrids are connected to the main power grid, and are also interconnected with each other. These interconnected microgrids can exchange power and information with each other through a power bus and a communication network. Fig. 1 illustrates such a system model."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈ M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determine the amount of energy trading e_i = {e_{i,j}^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i} and the associated payment π_i = {π_{i,j}, ∀j ∈ M\\i}. Here e_{i,j}^t denotes the amount of energy that microgrid i exchanges with microgrid j in time slot t, and π_{i,j} denotes the associated payment for energy trading between microgrid i and microgrid j. If microgrid i purchases energy from microgrid j in time slot t, then e_{i,j}^t > 0; otherwise, microgrid i sells energy to microgrid j and e_{i,j}^t \u003c 0. Similarly, if microgrid i makes payment to microgrid j, then π_{i,j} > 0; otherwise microgrid i receives payment from microgrid j and π_{i,j} \u003c 0. 4Note that the payments π_i are determined through bargaining between microgrids, and are not necessary linear in terms of the energy exchange e_i."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"BP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy d- trading & scheduling and trading p","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"P1: Social operating cost minimization problem:\nmin ∑_{i∈M} CO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i})\nsubject to (1)–(3), (5), (6), (8)–(11), (14), (15), and (17),\nvariables: { g_i, q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}, e_i, i ∈ M }.\nSolving P1 determines the optimal energy trading & scheduling {g_i^*, q_i^*, x_n^*, r_{c,i}^*, r_{d,i}^*, e_i^*} for each microgrid i ∈ M. If a microgrid i has a nonzero energy trading vector e_i, then it belongs to set M′, and will participate in the payment bargaining in Problem P2. For the non-trading microgrids j ∈ M\\M′, they do not participate in the payment bargaining and their operating costs are the same as the disagreement points C_j^{Non}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SMART GRID, VOL. 9, NO. 4, JULY 2018 he thus each such microgrid i achieves the","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"thus each such microgrid i achieves the same performance of C_i^{Non} as in Section III-B, when they operate separately from other microgrids. Therefore, only those microgrids in set M′ that trade energy with others contribute to the total cost reduction of the system."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-participation","v141-a-M01-pricing","v141-a-M01-validation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","quote":"The only criterion for determining trading and non-trading microgrids is whether they can contribute to the social cost minimization."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","quote":"In the first step, we solve a social operating cost minimization problem P1 for the interconnected-microgrids system, which yields the optimal energy scheduling and energy trading for all microgrids. Those microgrids that trade energy with other microgrids form the set M′."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"0f2a0baf382080144107f0fd42f973d284baa329f869c32e9b1d4e50a699a845","applicability":"formalized","reason":"Applicability is formalized. The source defines the trading set M′ as exactly those microgrids that trade energy with other microgrids, and states that a microgrid belongs to M′ iff its P1 optimal inter-microgrid energy trading vector e_i is nonzero (E17, E18, E20, E21). The formal expression M′ = {i ∈ M | e_i ≠ 0} is an equivalent translation of this stated criterion. The frozen graph does not itself carry variable nodes for M, M′, or e_i; these are declared here as rule-local x/theta objects and bound to the existing conditional edge v141-c-R005-E001 linking matching.counterparties and clearing.social_welfare. All attached B evidence is unreviewed candidate material (no independent source audit performed); no verified-source-correctness claim is made.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","node_ids":["matching.counterparties","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R005-E001"],"rationale":"Formalizes the conditional edge: the matching set M′ is exactly the set of microgrids whose P1 optimal inter-microgrid trading vector e_i is nonzero. This is an equivalent translation of the source criterion (E18: nonzero e_i implies membership in M′; E17 and E21: the only criterion for trading vs non-trading is contribution to social cost minimization; E20: non-trading microgrids do not contribute). Because e_i is determined by the P1 social-welfare clearing outcome, the matching node depends on the clearing.social_welfare node."},{"object_id":"e_i","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","matching.counterparties"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R005-E001"],"rationale":"e_i is the P1 inter-microgrid energy trading vector. Its nonzero value is the source-stated membership criterion for M′. The variable therefore connects the social-welfare clearing node (where e_i is determined) to the matching node (where membership is classified) via the conditional edge."},{"object_id":"M_prime","node_ids":["matching.counterparties","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R005-E001"],"rationale":"M′ is the trading group output of the matching rule; it is defined as the set of microgrids with nonzero P1 trading vector e_i, so it is determined by the social-welfare clearing outcome and feeds the matching classification."},{"object_id":"M","node_ids":["matching.counterparties","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R005-E001"],"rationale":"M is the set of interconnected microgrids over which the matching condition is quantified and over which P1 social operating cost minimization is defined. It provides the scope for both nodes in the conditional relation."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
